  2976'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-128


                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  2977'                         EXADDR:
  2977'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2978'   32 2FC5'              	STA	SRNGF
  297B'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  297C'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  297E'   CA 29A7'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  2981'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  2982'   CD 29BC'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2985'   DA 29A5'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2988'   22 2FC6'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  298B'   22 2FC8'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  298E'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  298F'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  2991'   C2 299E'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  2994'   13                    	INX	D
  2995'   CD 29BC'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2998'   DA 29A5'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  299B'   22 2FC8'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  299E'                         EXA_YS:
  299E'   EB                    	XCHG
  299F'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29A1'   32 2FC5'              	STA	SRNGF
  29A4'   C9                    	RET
  29A5'                         EXA_NO:
  29A5'   EB                    	XCHG
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-129


  29A6'   C9                    	RET
  29A7'                         EXA_AL:
  29A7'   23                    	INX	H
  29A8'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  29A9'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  29AC'   22 2FC6'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  29AF'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  29B2'   22 2FC8'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  29B5'   E1                    	POP	H
  29B6'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29B8'   32 2FC5'              	STA	SRNGF
  29BB'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  29BC'                         EXNUM:
  29BC'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  29BD'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  29BF'   D8                    	RC
  29C0'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  29C2'   3F                    	CMC
  29C3'   D8                    	RC
  29C4'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  29C7'                         EXN_LP:
  29C7'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  29C8'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  29CA'   DA 29E4'              	JC	EXN_DN
  29CD'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  29CF'   D2 29E4'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  29D2'   13                    	INX	D
  29D3'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  29D4'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  29D5'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  29D7'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  29D8'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  29D9'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  29DA'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  29DB'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  29DC'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  29DD'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  29DE'   D1                    	POP	D
  29DF'   09                    	DAD	B
  29E0'   C1                    	POP	B
  29E1'   C3 29C7'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  29E4'                         EXN_DN:
  29E4'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  29E5'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  29E6'                         EX_S:
  29E6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  29E7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29E8'   CA 2A2A'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  29EB'   32 2FCA'              	STA	SDELIM
  29EE'   23                    	INX	H
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-130


  29EF'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  29F2'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  29F5'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  29F6'   CD 2A30'              	CALL	SCOPY
  29F9'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  29FA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29FB'   CA 2A2A'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  29FE'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  2A01'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A03'   32 2F92'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  2A06'   11 2FDC'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  2A09'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  2A0B'   CD 2A30'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2A0E'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2A0F'   32 2FDB'              	STA	SREPLN
  2A12'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A13'   32 2FCB'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2A16'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2A17'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  2A19'   C2 2A22'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2A1C'   23                    	INX	H
  2A1D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A1F'   32 2FCB'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2A22'                         EX_SG:
  2A22'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2A23'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A24'   C2 27B1'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  2A27'   C3 2A50'              	JMP	SUBGO
  2A2A'                         SUBNOP:
  2A2A'   21 2879'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2A2D'   C3 27BF'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  2A30'                         SCOPY:
  2A30'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2A32'                         SCP_LP:
  2A32'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2A33'   B9                    	CMP	C
  2A34'   D2 2A4D'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  2A37'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2A38'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A39'   CA 2A4D'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  2A3C'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2A3D'   21 2FCA'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  2A40'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2A41'   E1                    	POP	H
  2A42'   CA 2A4C'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  2A45'   12                    	STAX	D
  2A46'   13                    	INX	D
  2A47'   23                    	INX	H
  2A48'   04                    	INR	B
  2A49'   C3 2A32'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  2A4C'                         SCP_SK:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-131


  2A4C'   23                    	INX	H
  2A4D'                         SCP_DN:
  2A4D'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A4E'   12                    	STAX	D
  2A4F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  2A50'                         SUBGO:
  2A50'   CD 13CD'              	CALL	GZONE
  2A53'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2A56'   22 2FD9'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2A59'   21 2FD6'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  2A5C'   CD 0429'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  2A5F'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2A60'   32 2FCC'              	STA	SANY
  2A63'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2A66'   22 2FCE'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2A69'   3A 2FC5'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2A6C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A6D'   CA 2A7F'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  2A70'   CD 233F'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  2A73'   DA 2AFB'              	JC	SUBBAD
  2A76'   2A 2FC6'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  2A79'   CD 133B'              	CALL	GOLN
  2A7C'   C3 2A85'              	JMP	SUBLP
  2A7F'                         SUB_1L:
  2A7F'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2A82'   22 2FD0'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2A85'                         SUBLP:
  2A85'   CD 2B01'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2A88'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A89'   CA 2A97'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  2A8C'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A8E'   32 2FCC'              	STA	SANY
  2A91'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2A94'   22 2FCE'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2A97'                         SUB_NX:
  2A97'   2A 2FD0'              	LHLD	SLINES
  2A9A'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2A9B'   22 2FD0'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2A9E'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2A9F'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2AA0'   CA 2ABE'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  2AA3'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2AA6'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2AA7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  2AAA'   3A 2FCC'              	LDA	SANY
  2AAD'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AAE'   CA 2AB8'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  2AB1'   2A 2FCE'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2AB4'   23                    	INX	H
  2AB5'   22 2FCE'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-132


                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  2AB8'                         SUB_AE:
  2AB8'   CD 0BD7'              	CALL	ATEOF
  2ABB'   D2 2A85'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  2ABE'                         SUBDON:
  2ABE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  2AC1'   3A 2FCC'              	LDA	SANY
  2AC4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AC5'   CA 2AE3'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  2AC8'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2ACA'   32 2F06'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  2ACD'   2A 2FCE'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2AD0'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2AD1'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2AD2'   CA 2ADD'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  2AD5'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  2AD7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  2ADA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  2ADD'                         SUB_PL:
  2ADD'   CD 13E5'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  2AE0'   C3 0898'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  2AE3'                         SUBNFD:
  2AE3'   21 2FD6'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  2AE6'   CD 03D0'              	CALL	BACKTO
  2AE9'   2A 2FD9'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  2AEC'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  2AEF'   21 2849'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2AF2'   CD 27BF'              	CALL	EXERR
  2AF5'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2AF8'   C3 27BA'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  2AFB'                         SUBBAD:
  2AFB'   21 2837'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  2AFE'   C3 27B4'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  2B01'                         SUBLIN:
  2B01'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2B02'   32 2FCD'              	STA	SLCHG
  2B05'   CD 2B80'              	CALL	SUBBOL
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-133


  2B08'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  2B0B'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  2B0B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  2B0E'   DA 2B16'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  2B11'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  2B13'   C2 2B0B'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  2B16'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  2B16'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2B19'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B1A'   CD 19C0'              	CALL	CEND
  2B1D'   D1                    	POP	D
  2B1E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  2B21'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  2B22'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2B25'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2B26'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2B27'   FA 2B79'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  2B2A'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  2B2B'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  2B2C'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2B2F'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  2B32'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2B35'   DA 2B79'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2B38'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  2B3B'   EB                    	XCHG
  2B3C'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  2B3F'   EB                    	XCHG
  2B40'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2B43'   EB                    	XCHG
  2B44'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2B45'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2B48'   D1                    	POP	D
  2B49'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  2B4A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  2B4D'   3A 2FCC'              	LDA	SANY
  2B50'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B51'   C2 2B5E'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2B54'   3A 2FCD'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2B57'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B58'   C2 2B5E'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2B5B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  2B5E'                         SUBL_C:
  2B5E'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  2B61'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2B62'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2B64'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2B67'   11 2FDB'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-134


  2B6A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  2B6D'                         SUBL_I:
  2B6D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2B6F'   32 2FCD'              	STA	SLCHG
  2B72'   3A 2FCB'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2B75'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B76'   C2 2B16'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2B79'                         SUBL_D:
  2B79'   CD 2B80'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2B7C'   3A 2FCD'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2B7F'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  2B80'                         SUBBOL:
  2B80'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2B83'   CD 0678'              	CALL	BOL
  2B86'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2B89'                         VC_SRCH:
  2B89'   CD 085B'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2B8C'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2B8D'   CD 0786'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2B90'   22 2FBB'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2B93'   C1                    	POP	B
  2B94'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2B95'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2B97'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2B99'   CA 2B9E'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2B9C'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2B9E'                         VS_DIR:
  2B9E'   32 2F90'              	STA	SDIR
  2BA1'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2BA4'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2BA7'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2BA8'   CD 20E8'              	CALL	RDLINE
  2BAB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BAC'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  2BAD'   3A 2F67'              	LDA	RLLEN
  2BB0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BB1'   CA 2BC6'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2BB4'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2BB5'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2BB7'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2BBA'   19                    	DAD	D
  2BBB'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  2BBD'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  2BBE'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  2BC1'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2BC3'   32 2F92'              	STA	SHAVE
  2BC6'                         VS_OLD:
  2BC6'   3A 2F92'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2BC9'   B7                    	ORA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-135


  2BCA'   CA 2BD6'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  2BCD'   3A 2F90'              	LDA	SDIR
  2BD0'   32 2F91'              	STA	SDIRE
  2BD3'   C3 2BFF'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2BD6'                         VS_NOP:
  2BD6'   21 2879'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2BD9'   C3 27BF'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  2BDC'                         VC_N:
  2BDC'   CD 085B'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2BDF'   3A 2F90'              	LDA	SDIR
  2BE2'   C3 2BEC'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2BE5'                         VCB_N:
  2BE5'   CD 085B'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2BE8'   3A 2F90'              	LDA	SDIR
  2BEB'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  2BEC'                         VN_GO:
  2BEC'   32 2F91'              	STA	SDIRE
  2BEF'   CD 0786'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2BF2'   22 2FBB'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2BF5'   3A 2F92'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2BF8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BF9'   CA 2BD6'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  2BFC'   C3 2BFF'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  2BFF'                         SRCHN:
  2BFF'   CD 13CD'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2C02'   CD 07AF'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and an ESC may stop it
  2C05'                         SN_LP:
  2C05'   CD 2C3A'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2C08'   DA 2C25'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2C0B'   2A 2FBB'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2C0E'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2C0F'   22 2FBB'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2C12'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2C13'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2C14'   C2 2C05'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2C17'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2C1A'   CD 07EC'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2C1D'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2C1F'   32 2F9D'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2C22'   C3 13E5'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2C25'                         SN_BAD:
  2C25'   CD 07EC'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2C28'   DA 0804'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  2C2B'   CD 07F7'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2C2E'   21 2849'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2C31'   CD 27BF'              	CALL	EXERR
  2C34'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-136


  2C37'   C3 27BA'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2C3A'                         SRCHGO:
  2C3A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2C3C'   32 2FC4'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  2C3F'   CD 2C55'              	CALL	SRCHW
  2C42'   D0                    	RNC
  2C43'   CD 07F0'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2C46'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2C47'   3A 2F91'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2C4A'   2F                    	CMA
  2C4B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2C4E'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2C4F'   32 2FC4'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  2C52'   C3 2C55'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2C55'                         SRCHW:
  2C55'   3A 2F91'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2C58'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2C5B'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2C5E'   22 2F03'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  2C61'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2C62'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2C65'                         SW_LP:
  2C65'   CD 07F0'              	CALL	BRKQ		; an ESC spent the count, here or on the way to
  2C68'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2C69'   CD 2C75'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2C6C'   D0                    	RNC
  2C6D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2C70'   D2 2C65'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2C73'   37                    	STC
  2C74'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2C75'                         SRCH1:
  2C75'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2C78'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2C7B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C7C'   FA 2CA0'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2C7F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2C82'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-137


  2C85'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2C88'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2C89'   3A 2FC4'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2C8C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C8D'   CA 2C96'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2C90'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2C91'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2C92'   37                    	STC
  2C93'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2C94'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2C95'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2C96'                         SR1_FW:
  2C96'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2C99'   D8                    	RC
  2C9A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2C9D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2CA0'                         SR1_BK:
  2CA0'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2CA3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2CA6'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2CA9'   D8                    	RC
  2CAA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2CAD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2CB0'   D8                    	RC
  2CB1'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2CB4'                         MSGNAM:
  2CB4'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2CB5'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2CB7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2CBA'   E1                    	POP	H
  2CBB'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-138


  2CBC'   23                    	INX	H
  2CBD'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2CBE'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2CC0'   C2 2CCD'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2CC3'   21 28E7'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2CC6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2CC9'   E1                    	POP	H
  2CCA'   C3 2D17'              	JMP	MN_END
  2CCD'                         MN_HAS:
  2CCD'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2CCF'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2CD2'   E1                    	POP	H
  2CD3'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2CD4'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2CD5'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2CD6'   CA 2CE4'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2CD9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2CDA'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2CDC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2CDF'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2CE1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2CE4'                         MN_NAM:
  2CE4'   E1                    	POP	H
  2CE5'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2CE6'   23                    	INX	H
  2CE7'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  2CE9'                         MN_NM:
  2CE9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2CEA'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2CEC'   CA 2CF7'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2CEF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2CF2'   23                    	INX	H
  2CF3'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2CF4'   C2 2CE9'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  2CF7'                         MN_EXT:
  2CF7'   E1                    	POP	H
  2CF8'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  2CFB'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  2CFC'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2CFD'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2CFF'   CA 2D17'              	JZ	MN_END
  2D02'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2D04'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2D07'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2D09'                         MN_XL:
  2D09'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2D0A'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2D0C'   CA 2D17'              	JZ	MN_END
  2D0F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2D12'   23                    	INX	H
  2D13'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2D14'   C2 2D09'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2D17'                         MN_END:
  2D17'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2D19'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-139


                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2D1C'                         MSGFLG:
  2D1C'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2D1E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2D1F'   CA 2D2F'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2D22'   3A 2F06'              	LDA	MODF
  2D25'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2D26'   CA 2D2F'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2D29'   21 28F1'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2D2C'   CD 2D44'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2D2F'                         MF_RO:
  2D2F'   3A 2F7D'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2D32'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2D33'   CA 2D3C'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2D36'   21 28FC'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2D39'   CD 2D44'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2D3C'                         MF_NEW:
  2D3C'   3A 2F7B'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2D3F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2D40'   C8                    	RZ
  2D41'   21 2907'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2D44'                         MF_PUT:
  2D44'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2D45'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2D46'   C2 2D52'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2D49'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2D4B'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2D4C'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2D4E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2D51'   E1                    	POP	H
  2D52'                         MF_STR:
  2D52'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2D55'                         EMITW:
  2D55'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2D56'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2D57'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2D59'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2D5C'   CD 2D7A'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2D5F'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2D62'   CD 2D7A'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2D65'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2D68'   CD 2D7A'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2D6B'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2D6E'   CD 2D7A'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2D71'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2D72'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2D74'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2D77'   D1                    	POP	D
  2D78'   C1                    	POP	B
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-140


  2D79'   C9                    	RET
  2D7A'                         EWDIG:
  2D7A'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2D7C'                         EW_LP:
  2D7C'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2D7D'   93                    	SUB	E
  2D7E'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2D7F'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2D80'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2D81'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2D82'   DA 2D89'              	JC	EW_DN
  2D85'   0C                    	INR	C
  2D86'   C3 2D7C'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2D89'                         EW_DN:
  2D89'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2D8A'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2D8B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2D8C'   C2 2D92'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2D8F'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2D90'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2D91'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2D92'                         EW_PR:
  2D92'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2D93'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2D95'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2D98'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2D9A'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2D9B'                         ISEMPT:
  2D9B'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2D9E'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2D9F'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2DA0'   C0                    	RNZ
  2DA1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2DA4'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2DA5'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2DA6'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2DA7'                         MSGCTG:
  2DA7'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2DAA'   CD 2CB4'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2DAD'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2DAF'   CD 2D1C'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2DB2'   CD 2D9B'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2DB5'   C2 2DBE'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2DB8'   21 291A'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2DBB'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2DBE'                         MG_POS:
  2DBE'   21 290D'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2DC1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-141


  2DC4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2DC7'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2DC8'   CD 2D55'              	CALL	EMITW
  2DCB'   21 2914'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2DCE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2DD1'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2DD4'   23                    	INX	H
  2DD5'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2DD6'   CD 2D55'              	CALL	EMITW
  2DD9'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2DDC'   23                    	INX	H
  2DDD'   D1                    	POP	D
  2DDE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2DE1'   C8                    	RZ
  2DE2'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2DE3'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2DE5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2DE8'   E1                    	POP	H
  2DE9'   C3 2D55'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2DEC'                         FILMSG:
  2DEC'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2DEF'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2DF1'   C8                    	RZ
  2DF2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2DF5'   CD 2CB4'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2DF8'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  2DF9'   C3 2D1C'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  2DFC'                         WRMSG:
  2DFC'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2DFF'   CD 2CB4'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2E02'   21 292C'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2E05'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2E08'                         MSGPST:
  2E08'   3A 2F7A'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2E0B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2E0C'   C8                    	RZ
  2E0D'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2E0E'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2E0F'   32 2F7A'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2E12'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2E13'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2E15'   CA 2E31'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2E18'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2E1A'   CA 2E37'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2E1D'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-142


  2E1F'   CA 2E28'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2E22'   CD 2DA7'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2E25'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2E28'                         MP_BIG:
  2E28'   21 28C8'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  2E2B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2E2E'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2E31'                         MP_FIL:
  2E31'   CD 2DEC'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2E34'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2E37'                         MP_WRT:
  2E37'   CD 2DFC'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2E3A'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2E3D'                         VC_CTLG:
  2E3D'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2E3F'   32 2F7A'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2E42'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2E44'   32 2F07'              	STA	RINTENT
  2E47'   C3 0786'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  2E4A'                         CMDTAB:
  2E4A'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  2E4B'   2E3D'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  2E4D'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2E4E'   08D3'                 	DW	VC_H
  2E50'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  2E51'   08FD'                 	DW	VC_L
  2E53'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  2E54'   09CE'                 	DW	VC_J
  2E56'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  2E57'   09E0'                 	DW	VC_K
  2E59'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2E5A'   0930'                 	DW	VC_0
  2E5C'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2E5D'   094E'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2E5F'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2E60'   098C'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2E62'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2E63'   09B0'                 	DW	VC_CR
  2E65'   77                    	DB	'w'
  2E66'   0C59'                 	DW	VC_W
  2E68'   62                    	DB	'b'
  2E69'   0CAA'                 	DW	VC_B
  2E6B'   65                    	DB	'e'
  2E6C'   0E40'                 	DW	VC_E
  2E6E'   57                    	DB	'W'
  2E6F'   0C60'                 	DW	VCBGW
  2E71'   42                    	DB	'B'
  2E72'   0CB1'                 	DW	VCBGB
  2E74'   45                    	DB	'E'
  2E75'   0E47'                 	DW	VCBGE
  2E77'   47                    	DB	'G'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-143


  2E78'   130B'                 	DW	VC_G
  2E7A'   67                    	DB	'g'
  2E7B'   135E'                 	DW	VC_LG
  2E7D'   78                    	DB	'x'
  2E7E'   1955'                 	DW	VC_X
  2E80'   5A                    	DB	'Z'
  2E81'   215B'                 	DW	VC_BZ
  2E83'   66                    	DB	'f'
  2E84'   021A'                 	DW	VC_FF
  2E86'   46                    	DB	'F'
  2E87'   021F'                 	DW	VC_FB
  2E89'   74                    	DB	't'
  2E8A'   0224'                 	DW	VC_TF
  2E8C'   54                    	DB	'T'
  2E8D'   0229'                 	DW	VC_TB
  2E8F'   3B                    	DB	';'
  2E90'   0240'                 	DW	VC_SEMI
  2E92'   2C                    	DB	','
  2E93'   024A'                 	DW	VC_COMMA
  2E95'   79                    	DB	'y'
  2E96'   1C65'                 	DW	VC_Y
  2E98'   59                    	DB	'Y'
  2E99'   1C6D'                 	DW	VY_YY
  2E9B'   70                    	DB	'p'
  2E9C'   1EA3'                 	DW	VC_P
  2E9E'   50                    	DB	'P'
  2E9F'   1EB8'                 	DW	VCB_P
  2EA1'   69                    	DB	'i'
  2EA2'   19E1'                 	DW	VC_I
  2EA4'   61                    	DB	'a'
  2EA5'   19E7'                 	DW	VC_A
  2EA7'   41                    	DB	'A'
  2EA8'   1A07'                 	DW	VCB_A
  2EAA'   49                    	DB	'I'
  2EAB'   1A23'                 	DW	VCB_I
  2EAD'   72                    	DB	'r'
  2EAE'   1B65'                 	DW	VC_R
  2EB0'   52                    	DB	'R'
  2EB1'   1BBE'                 	DW	VCB_R
  2EB3'   64                    	DB	'd'
  2EB4'   1BD3'                 	DW	VC_D
  2EB6'   44                    	DB	'D'
  2EB7'   193A'                 	DW	VCB_D
  2EB9'   43                    	DB	'C'
  2EBA'   1934'                 	DW	VCB_C
  2EBC'   63                    	DB	'c'
  2EBD'   1BDB'                 	DW	VC_C
  2EBF'   6F                    	DB	'o'
  2EC0'   1A71'                 	DW	VC_O
  2EC2'   4F                    	DB	'O'
  2EC3'   1A86'                 	DW	VCB_O
  2EC5'   4A                    	DB	'J'
  2EC6'   1AA0'                 	DW	VCB_J
  2EC8'   7E                    	DB	'~'
  2EC9'   1B22'                 	DW	VC_TILD
  2ECB'   3A                    	DB	':'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-144


  2ECC'   2186'                 	DW	VC_EX
  2ECE'   2F                    	DB	'/'
  2ECF'   2B89'                 	DW	VC_SRCH
  2ED1'   3F                    	DB	'?'
  2ED2'   2B89'                 	DW	VC_SRCH
  2ED4'   6E                    	DB	'n'
  2ED5'   2BDC'                 	DW	VC_N
  2ED7'   4E                    	DB	'N'
  2ED8'   2BE5'                 	DW	VCB_N
  2EDA'   48                    	DB	'H'
  2EDB'   0F04'                 	DW	VC_HIGH
  2EDD'   4D                    	DB	'M'
  2EDE'   0F1B'                 	DW	VC_MIDL
  2EE0'   4C                    	DB	'L'
  2EE1'   0F36'                 	DW	VC_LOWL
  2EE3'   06                    	DB	06H			; ^F -- page forward
  2EE4'   0F53'                 	DW	VC_PGDN
  2EE6'   02                    	DB	02H			; ^B -- page back
  2EE7'   0FB1'                 	DW	VC_PGUP
  2EE9'   04                    	DB	04H			; ^D -- half page down
  2EEA'   1092'                 	DW	VC_HFDN
  2EEC'   15                    	DB	15H			; ^U -- half page up
  2EED'   1159'                 	DW	VC_HFUP
  2EEF'   0C                    	DB	0CH			; ^L -- redraw
  2EF0'   1308'                 	DW	VC_REDR
  2EF2'   2E                    	DB	'.'			; '.' -- repeat the last change
  2EF3'   017D'                 	DW	VC_DOT
  2EF5'   75                    	DB	'u'			; 'u' -- undo (and undo the undo)
  2EF6'   0545'                 	DW	VC_UNDO
  2EF8'   6D                    	DB	'm'		; m{a-c} -- set a mark
  2EF9'   032E'                 	DW	VC_MK
  2EFB'   60                    	DB	60H		; `{a-c} -- back to its exact spot
  2EFC'   0333'                 	DW	VC_BQ
  2EFE'   27                    	DB	27H		; '{a-c} -- back to its line's first non-blank
  2EFF'   0338'                 	DW	VC_AQ
  2F01'   00                    	DB	0
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DATA
                                ;============================================================
  2F02'   00                    EDMODE: DB	EM_CMD
  2F03'   0000                  CMDCNT: DW	0		; count prefix (0 = none)
  2F05'   00                    QUITF:	DB	0		; set 1 by :q / :wq
  2F06'   00                    MODF:	DB	0		; 1 = unsaved changes
  2F07'   00                    RINTENT:DB	R_FULL		; render intent for the last key (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2F08'   01                    ILN:	DB	1		; lines an R_ILIN paint opens (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2F09'   00                    ICHAPP:	DB	0		; 1 = the R_ICH char went at the row's end, so
                                				;   nothing follows it to shift: no ICH needed
  2F0A'   01                    DCHN:	DB	1		; the cells an R_DCH paint deletes (SCRN.MAC reads)
  2F0B'   00                    DCHBS:	DB	0		; 1 = they went left of the cursor (insert's BS)
  2F0C'   00                    LNALL:	DB	0		; 1 = this R_LINE is drawn from the line's first
                                				;   row, not the cursor's (SCRN.MAC clears it)
  2F0D'   00                    ILABOV:	DB	0		; 1 = the row above an R_ILIN's opened rows lost
                                				;   text to them and must be redrawn too
  2F0E'   FFFE                  GOALCOL:DW	0FFFEH		; sticky goal screen column for j/k (0FFFFH = EOL, 0FFFEH = stale)
  2F10'   00                    WBIG:	DB	0		; word motion: 0 = w/b/e, 1 = W/B/E
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-145


  2F11'   00                    WPULL:	DB	0		; w/W ended off a line's end and was pulled back
  2F12'   00                    HFAMT:	DB	0		; ^D/^U: rows (vim's 'scroll'); 0 = NEDIT/2
  2F13'   00                    HFN:	DB	0		; ^D/^U: the rows this one goes
  2F14'   0000                  HFTP:	DW	0		; ^D/^U: -> the new top line's entry
  2F16'   00                    HTN:	DB	0		; row table: its entries
  2F17'   00                    HTCUR:	DB	0		; ... the cursor line's
  2F18'   00                    HTTP:	DB	0		; ... the top line's
  2F19'   00                    HTEOF:	DB	0		; ... 1 = its last is the file's last line
  2F1A'   00                    HTBOF:	DB	0		; ... 1 = its first is the file's first
  2F1B'   0000                  RWR:	DW	0		; ^F/^B: rows still to go
  2F1D'   00                    RWMV:	DB	0		; ^F/^B: 1 = the table was made again on the way
  2F1E'   00                    OVDIR:	DB	0		; OVLAP: 0 = h2.. are above h1 in the table
  2F1F'   0000                  JKCNT:	DW	0		; j/k: count
  2F21'   0000                  JKN:	DW	0		; j/k: lines scanned for (min(count, limit))
  2F23'   0000                  JKMOV:	DW	0		; j: lines moved
  2F25'   0000                  JKBK:	DW	0		; k: scan position, bytes back from GAPBEG
  2F27'   00                    JKPHN:	DB	0		; j: 1 = moved may be one less (last LF ends the file)
  2F28'   00                    JKLST:	DB	0		; j: last byte of the scan
  2F29'   0000                  JKVC:	DW	0		; j/k landing: screen column
  2F2B'   0000                  JKLS:	DW	0		; j/k landing: last content char (0FFFFH = none)
  0001                          PG	EQU	1		; pending-prefix codes held in GPEND/GSAVE
  0002                          PZ	EQU	2
  0003                          PR	EQU	3		;   ('r', whose next key is its operand)
  0004                          PM	EQU	4		; which of 'm' / '`' / ''' is running (MKWHAT, not
  0005                          PJ	EQU	5		;   GPEND: each reads its own mark letter, so none
  0006                          PJL	EQU	6		;   of them leaves anything pending)
                                ;--- '.' (repeat): see the '.' section above for how these are driven.
  2F2D'   00                    DOTCHG: DB	0		; 1 = the command being typed changed the text
  2F2E'   01                    DOTSTA: DB	1		; 1 = the next key starts a new recording
  2F2F'   00                    DOTRPL: DB	0		; 1 = '.' is replaying, so nothing is recorded
  2F30'   00                    DOTOVF: DB	0		; 1 = the change outgrew the scratch
  2F31'   00                    DOTLK:	DB	0		; the last key recorded, encoded
  2F32'   0000*                 DOTSP:	DW	DOTSCR		; the scratch's write pointer
  2F34'   00                    DOTLEN: DB	0		; keys in DOTBUF (0 = nothing to repeat)
  2F35'   00                    DOTIX:	DB	0		; the replay's read index into DOTBUF
  2F36'   0000                  DOTNCT: DW	0		; the count typed on the '.' (0 = none)
                                
  2F38'   00                    GPEND:	DB	0		; pending 'g' / 'Z' / 'r' (gg / ZZ / r{char})
  2F39'   00                    GSAVE:	DB	0		; the pending snapshot for the current command
  2F3A'   00                    PENDOP: DB	0		; pending operator ('d' for dd)
  2F3B'   00                    PSAVE:	DB	0		; pending-op snapshot for the current command
  2F3C'   00                    DDSNG:	DB	0		; 1 = the current dd deletes a single line (DL paint)
  2F3D'   00                    OPCLSV: DB	0		; operator: motion class (0 excl, 1 incl, 2 linewise,
                                				;   3 excl but a jump -- no end-of-line clamp)
  2F3E'   00                    CWEOL:	DB	0		; operator: the motion is 'cw' rewritten to 'ce',
                                				;   and its first word is yet to be stepped
  2F3F'   0000                  OPSTA:	DW	0		; operator: span start (cursor before the motion)
  2F41'   0000                  OPEND2: DW	0		; operator: span end (cursor after the motion)
  2F43'   00 00 00              OPSFP:	DB	0,0,0		; operator: the start as a place in the file
  2F46'   00 00 00              OPSLO:	DB	0,0,0		; ... where its line starts
  2F49'   00 00 00              OPSHI:	DB	0,0,0		; ... where the next line does (OPPGD: the span's
                                				;   high end, whichever line that turned out to be)
  2F4C'   00 00 00              OPTOGO: DB	0,0,0		; OPPGD: bytes still to take
  2F4F'   0000                  OPLO:	DW	0		; operator: low offset of the delete range
  2F51'   0000                  OPHI:	DW	0		; operator: high offset of the delete range
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-146


  2F53'   0000                  CNTV:	DW	0		; motion repeat counter
  2F55'   0000                  TTI:	DW	0		; TAILTAB scan index
  2F57'   0000                  WKI:	DW	0		; scan index
  2F59'   0000                  WKTERM: DW	0		; line terminator offset
  2F5B'   0000                  WKBOL:	DW	0		; EOLC scratch: line BOL
  2F5D'   00                    WKCLS:	DB	0		; word motion: class of the current run
  2F5E'   00                    VRCH:	DB	0		; 'r' / R: the char being stored
  2F5F'   0000                  RPDEL:	DW	0		; R: chars overwritten, still in the gap (BS puts back)
  2F61'   0000                  RPAPP:	DW	0		; R: chars appended past the line end (BS deletes)
  2F63'   0000                  RPGE:	DW	0		; R: GAPEND as the last overwrite left it
                                
                                
  2F65'   0000                  RLPTR:	DW	0		; RDLINE: write pointer
  2F67'   00                    RLLEN:	DB	0		; RDLINE: chars stored
  2F68'   00                    RLMAX:	DB	0		; RDLINE: capacity
  2F69'   00                    EXOP:	DB	0		; ex: the command (EO_xx)
  2F6A'   00                    EXARG:	DB	0		; ex: 0 = no file name given
  2F6B'   00                    EXBAK:	DB	0		; ex: the write keeps a backup
  2F6C'   0000                  EXCMD:	DW	0		; ex: text shown after a message
  2F6E'   00                    WPROW:	DB	0		; :w -- the cursor's row
  2F6F'   00                    WPBYT:	DB	0		; :w -- the cursor's byte in its record
  2F70'   0000                  WPREC:	DW	0		; :w -- the cursor's record in the file
  2F72'   0000                  ZDIF:	DW	0		; G/gg: target - top (low 16 bits)
  2F74'   00                    ZLF:	DB	0		; G/gg: lines from the target up to the top
  2F75'   0000                  ZSVCNT: DW	0		; G/gg: CMDCNT around LINPSB
  2F77'   0000                  ZSVAUX: DW	0		; G/gg: AUXCNT around LINPSB
  2F79'   00                    ZSVDIR: DB	0		; G/gg: DIRFLG around LINPSB
  2F7A'   00                    MSGWHT: DB	0		; the message the bottom row still owes (MW_xxx)
  2F7B'   00                    MSGNEW: DB	0		; the file was not there when it was opened ('[New]')
  2F7C'   00                    EXBANG: DB	0		; ex: 0 = '!' given
  2F7D'   00                    ROFLAG: DB	0		; command line: '-R' / '/R' was given
  2F7E'   00                    PLUSF:	DB	0		; command line: '+' was given
  2F7F'   00                    YMODF:	DB	0		; yy: MODF across the delete + put back
  2F80'   00                    YDCHG:	DB	0		; yy: DOTCHG across it too (see '.')
  2F81'   00                    YURPL:	DB	0		; yy: UNDRPL across it too (see 'u')
  2F82'   0000                  YOFF:	DW	0		; yy: the cursor, which a yank leaves alone
  2F84'   0000                  YPT:	DW	0		; yy: where the lines came from
  2F86'   0000                  PCNT:	DW	0		; p/P: copies still to put
  2F88'   00 00 00              PPFP:	DB	0,0,0		; p/P: the first line put, a file position
  2F8B'   0000                  PLFS:	DW	0		; p/P: the lines in the register
  2F8D'   00                    PCPY:	DB	0		; p/P: the copies, kept for the paint
  2F8E'   0000                  YLDLT:	DW	0		; yy: LNDLT across the delete + put back
                                
  2F90'   00                    SDIR:	DB	0		; the last '/' or '?' direction (FFH = back)
  2F91'   00                    SDIRE:	DB	0		; ... the direction THIS search runs in ('N')
  2F92'   00                    SHAVE:	DB	0		; nonzero once a pattern has been entered
  2F93'   00                    FNDKND: DB	0		; f/F/t/T: which find ';' repeats (0 = none yet)
  2F94'   00                    FNDCH:	DB	0		; ... and the character it looked for
  2F95'   00                    FNDRUN: DB	0		; the find THIS command is running (',' reverses)
  2F96'   00                    FNDSTP: DB	0		; how far along it starts: 2 for a 't' repeat
  2F97'   0000                  FNDN:	DW	0		; occurrences still to count off
  2F99'   0000                  FNDCUR: DW	0		; the cursor it started from
  2F9B'   0000                  FNDLIM: DW	0		; ... and the end of the line it may not pass
  2F9D'   00                    GPKEEP: DB	0		; GPLACE: keep the column (a search) not FNBLNK
  2F9E'   00 00 00              MKVAL:	DB	0,0,0		; marks: nonzero once 'm' has set that one.  It
                                				;   lives HERE and not in MKTAB because the image
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-147


                                				;   guarantees a DB, and nothing guarantees a DS
  2FA1'   00 00 00              MKEDIT: DB	0,0,0		; MKSHFT: where in the file the edit is
  2FA4'   0000                  MKN:	DW	0		; ... how long it is
  2FA6'   00                    MKTYP:	DB	0		; ... and which way: 1 text went in, 2 came out
  2FA7'   00 00 00              MKPOS:	DB	0,0,0		; MKJMP: the file position being jumped to
  2FAA'   00                    MKWHAT: DB	0		; which of 'm' / '`' / ''' is running
  2FAB'   00                    MKHOLD: DB	0		; set across YKGO's delete + put back (a yank
                                				;   that could not copy): MKSHFT must leave the
                                				;   marks alone through it
  2FAC'   0000                  YACT:	DW	0		; how this yank takes its lines (VD_DD / YSPAN)
  2FAE'   0000                  YTGT:	DW	0		; YSPAN: the text's length once the span is out
  2FB0'   0000                  YWAS:	DW	0		; ... and its length BEFORE the lines came out
  2FB2'   0000                  YCOL:	DW	0		; ... how far into them the cursor sat
  2FB4'   00 00 00              YPFP:	DB	0,0,0		; ... and where in the file they start
                                ;--- the yank that copies instead (YNCOPY / YNSPAN) ---
  2FB7'   0000                  YNPG:	DW	0		; the window the yank started in (YNDONE's paint)
  2FB9'   0000                  YNCNT:	DW	0		; YNLINE: the lines it still wants
  2FBB'   0000                  SCNT:	DW	0		; searches still to do (the count)
  2FBD'   00 00 00              SOFF:	DB	0,0,0		; BRKON: where in the FILE the command began
  2FC0'   0000                  SLDLT:	DW	0		; ... and LNDLT: for a search that fails, and
                                				;   any move an ESC stops
  2FC2'   00                    BRKARM: DB	0		; nonzero: CNTTST looks for an ESC
  2FC3'   00                    BRKHIT: DB	0		; nonzero: one was found, and the count spent
  2FC4'   00                    SSKIP:	DB	0		; skip the character under the cursor (not
                                				;   on the wrapped pass)
                                
  0024                          SREPMX	EQU	EXMAX-4		; ':s' -- bounded by the ex line itself, so
                                				;   SCOPY can never truncate a line anyone is
                                				;   able to type: the shortest possible
                                				;   substitute is 's' '/' + one pattern char +
                                				;   '/' before the replacement starts, and
                                				;   RDLINE stores at most EXMAX.  Tied to
                                				;   EXMAX rather than written out, the way
                                				;   BUF.MAC ties SRCMAX to SRMAX, so the two
                                				;   cannot drift apart.
  2FC5'   00                    SRNGF:	DB	0		; ex: a line range was given
  2FC6'   0000                  SRNG1:	DW	0		; ex: its first line
  2FC8'   0000                  SRNG2:	DW	0		; ... and its last (FFFFH = the file's last)
  2FCA'   00                    SDELIM: DB	0		; ':s' the delimiter typed
  2FCB'   00                    SGFLAG: DB	0		; ':s' the 'g' flag
  2FCC'   00                    SANY:	DB	0		; ':s' something in the range has changed
  2FCD'   00                    SLCHG:	DB	0		; ':s' this line changed
  2FCE'   0000                  SBACK:	DW	0		; ':s' lines walked past the last change
  2FD0'   0000                  SLINES: DW	0		; ':s' / ':N,Mw' lines of the range still to do
  2FD2'   00                    RWPTR:	DB	0		; ':N,Mw' the next free byte of the record at
                                				;   0080H, as that address's low byte
  2FD3'   00                    RWERR:	DB	0		; ':N,Mw' a record would not go: the disk is full
  2FD4'   00                    RDANY:	DB	0		; ':r' something has been read in
  2FD5'   00                    RDBRK:	DB	0		; ':r' the line above wants a line end first
  2FD6'   00 00 00              SOFFS:	DB	0,0,0		; ':s' the cursor's place in the file, for one that
  2FD9'   0000                  SLDLTS: DW	0		;   changes nothing, and its LNDLT
  2FDB'   00                    SREPLN: DB	0		; ':s' the replacement's length, kept
  2FDC'                         SREPL:	DS	SREPMX+1	;   just below it: WM's RDSTR layout,
                                				;   so INSSTR can splice it
                                
                                	END
